  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 321 AM EST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\n- Cooler today, with lake effect showers near the lakeshore and far \nnorth.\n\nNow that the cold front has passed through the area, attention \nturns to the establishment of lake effect rain (mostly) bands \nalong the immediate lakeshore and the northern lower peninsula. \nCold air advection has begun in earnest with north-northwesterly \ncyclonic flow, and 850 mb temperatures have fallen to between -2 \nand -4C already as of 3am, and may drop another 1-3 degrees over \nthe next few hours. With lake temperatures still quite warm \n(12-15C), this is producing a temperature difference of 14-16C, \nwhich may reach as high as 19C when the core of the cold air \nmoves overhead. Meanwhile, inversion heights are expected to rise \nto around 8k-9kft, and a prominent lake effect rain band has \nalready formed along the immediate lakeshore in an area of \nenhanced convergence. Further north, numerous additional lake \neffect bands are also forming downstream of the northern lakes, \nand one particular band emanating from Grand Traverse Bay may be \nrelevant to us later this morning.\n\nIn any case, these lake effect showers will mature this morning, \ncontinue through the day, and diminish gradually this evening. \nTemperatures currently are still too warm for snow where \nprecipitation is falling (low 40s near the lakeshore), and we \nremain confident that the vast majority of this will fall as rain,\nparticularly within the primary band along the lakeshore. \nInstead, expect another 1/4-1/2 inch of rain in western \nMason/Oceana counties, and possibly western Allegan/Van Buren \ncounties as well. Lighter amounts are also possible across our \nnorthern row of counties as the remnants of lake effect bands from\nthe northern lakes expand further downstream, but precipitation \nrates with these bands should be much lower than the lakeshore \nband. We may also be grazed in our northeast by more synoptically \ndriven, lake enhanced precipitation as the upper low moves along \nthe length of Lake Huron, but this would likely be very light as \nwell. \n\nWe will also need to keep an eye out for a few wet snowflakes with \nthis activity later this morning as cold air deepens and the upper \npart of the cloud layer cools to near -10C (enough for cloud ice \nformation). If this occurs at all, it will be most plausible in the \nhigher terrain of Lake/Osceola counties if the previously \nmentioned Grand Traverse Bay lake effect band can become \nestablished. Even if this occurs, about the most we could expect \nwith this band would be a thin layer of slushy snow almost \nexclusively on grassy surfaces, due to a combination of warm \nsurface temperatures, modest precipitation rates that far inland, \nand very low snow-liquid ratios. More likely is that snow will \nmelt before accumulating at all. \n\n- Much more typical temperatures and dry Saturday onward \n\nOnce lake effect showers diminish this evening, a dry pattern will \nbecome established for the foreseeable future, with little \nopportunity for precipitation of any variety. This weekend will see \ncool, but near seasonal average temperatures, with morning lows near \nor slightly below freezing both Saturday and Sunday, and highs \nrising from the low 50s Saturday to the mid/upper 50s Sunday. A \nmodest shortwave passes the area to the northeast Sunday, but again \nis not expected to produce precipitation. Transient upper ridging\nis expected to boost temperatures slightly above average \nearly/mid week. \n\nIn the longer range, ensemble guidance has trended toward the \ndevelopment of a longwave trough across the eastern CONUS late in \nthe week, opening the door for cooler air to return south into the \nGreat Lakes. Ensemble temperature spreads remain large during this \nperiod, but the overall trend back toward cooler (or at least near \naverage) temperatures is clear nonetheless.",
